*Part of my [Build in Public](https://dev.to/zaerohell) series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.*

*Repo: `zaerohell/content-automation` · 2026-10-22*

\#playadev #buildinpublic
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I work on &lt;a href="https://sengi.run/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=launch-2026-10" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sengi&lt;/a&gt;, a hosted runner service for GitHub Actions, and the Sengi rows below are ours. GitHub's figures are its published per-minute rates and included minutes, the ones in effect since January 1, 2026. Every line of arithmetic is on the page.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A GitHub Actions bill has two numbers in it: &lt;strong&gt;the minutes&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;the price per minute&lt;/strong&gt;. Most advice goes after the minutes: cache more, skip jobs, split suites. All good. But both numbers are also set by the machine your job runs on, and that's the lever this post is about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to work out your bill by hand
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Take last month's job-minutes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On standard runners in private repositories, subtract your plan's included minutes: 2,000 on GitHub Free, 3,000 on Pro and Team, 50,000 on Enterprise Cloud.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Multiply what's left by the runner's rate: $0.006 a minute for Linux 2-core, $0.012 for 4-core, $0.022 for 8-core.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Larger runners skip step 2. Included minutes never apply to them, so GitHub bills a 4- or 8-core job from its first minute. Each job is rounded up to a whole minute before the rate applies. Storage is a separate meter; leave it out, because it doesn't move when compute does.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Lever one: the price per minute
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sengi's matched sizes are $0.003, $0.006 and $0.012 a minute for 2, 4 and 8 vCPU, against GitHub's $0.006, $0.012 and $0.022. That's up to half the price for the same vCPU and memory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Lever two: the minutes themselves
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A hosted runner is a slice of a cloud server CPU on network storage. Sengi runs every job in a fresh VM on an AMD Ryzen 9 7950X3D: a desktop CPU boosting past 5 GHz, with DDR5 and local NVMe, on bare metal. Against GitHub's &lt;code&gt;ubuntu-latest&lt;/code&gt; at the same size, that gets &lt;strong&gt;up to 3×&lt;/strong&gt; the multi-core CPU, &lt;strong&gt;1.65×&lt;/strong&gt; the single-core and &lt;strong&gt;15×&lt;/strong&gt; the random-read disk speed. It's also the same CPU model on every job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That shows up as fewer minutes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Build&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;GitHub-hosted&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Sengi&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Faster&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Production Docker image (PostHog), 4 vCPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;14m36s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7m34s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.9×&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rust + TypeScript + Docker together, 4 vCPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;20m43s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;11m28s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.8×&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Node + Docker build (real CI), 2 vCPU&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;539 s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;299 s&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1.8×&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Multiply the two levers together and you get the price per build. That Docker image costs &lt;strong&gt;$0.175&lt;/strong&gt; on GitHub (14m36s at $0.012) and &lt;strong&gt;$0.045&lt;/strong&gt; on Sengi (7m34s at $0.006): &lt;strong&gt;74% less per build.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Three months, worked by hand
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Typical CI throughout: 1.8× faster on 2 vCPU and 1.81× on 4 vCPU, the measured speed-ups above. Sengi includes 3,000 free minutes a month, counted in 2 vCPU minutes, so they cover 1,500 minutes on 4 vCPU.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Under the quota: GitHub Free, 1,500 private minutes&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

