&lt;p&gt;The larger runner is where the gap is widest. GitHub bills every one of those minutes at $0.012, while Sengi finishes the same work in fewer minutes at half the rate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To run your own numbers, the &lt;a href="https://sengi.run/github-actions-cost-calculator?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=launch-2026-10" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cost calculator&lt;/a&gt; takes your plan, minutes, runner size and job type (typical CI, Docker, Rust or TypeScript). It runs in your browser with no email gate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Caches come along
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;actions/cache&lt;/code&gt;, the &lt;code&gt;setup-*&lt;/code&gt; actions' caches and Docker's &lt;code&gt;type=gha&lt;/code&gt; layer cache all work unchanged on Sengi, served from Sengi's own cache and included in the price. With a warm &lt;code&gt;type=gha&lt;/code&gt; cache, one measured Docker build step went from 68 s to 36 s.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Switching is one line
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install the Sengi GitHub App, connect the repository, then:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;jobs:
test:
-   runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+   runs-on: sengi-standard-2-ubuntu-2404
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;sengi-standard-4-ubuntu-2404&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;sengi-standard-8-ubuntu-2404&lt;/code&gt; give you 4 and 8 vCPU. Steps, secrets, triggers and logs stay in GitHub. 2 vCPU runners are pre-booted, so jobs start in about 2 seconds, and boot and teardown are never billed. Runners are Ubuntu 24.04 on x64. If you'd rather not touch the YAML, there's &lt;a href="https://sengi.run/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=launch-2026-10#agent" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;a prompt for your coding agent&lt;/a&gt; that finds every eligible job and opens the pull request for you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3,000 free minutes every month, no card:&lt;/strong&gt; point your slowest job at &lt;a href="https://sengi.run/?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=social&amp;amp;utm_campaign=launch-2026-10" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;sengi.run&lt;/a&gt; and compare its run time with last week's.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Synchronizing Terminal Workflows: Building &amp;amp; Deploying &lt;code&gt;severn-trace&lt;/code&gt; v0.1.2
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you work with autonomous AI agents or local development setups, you've likely hit the &lt;strong&gt;cross-platform dev gap&lt;/strong&gt;: your local Linux desktop setup works flawlessly, but your mobile or peripheral terminal (like Termux on Android) falls out of sync the moment you push new features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;severn-trace&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, our goal is simple: build a zero-dependency, lightweight diagnostic tool for AI agent telemetry and execution tracking that runs instantly on &lt;em&gt;any&lt;/em&gt; terminal workspace—from heavy Linux desktops to portable mobile environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Today, we officially released &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;severn-trace&lt;/code&gt; v0.1.2&lt;/strong&gt; on PyPI! 🎉&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a look at what's new in v0.1.2, how we revamped the console experience, and how we streamlined the release process across heterogeneous environments.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


💡 What's New in Version 0.1.2?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The headline feature of v0.1.2 is a total overhaul of the interactive command-line interface. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Expanded 9-Option Neon Console TUI
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We replaced simple linear flags with a full &lt;strong&gt;9-option neon-themed Terminal User Interface (TUI)&lt;/strong&gt;. The interface gives developers instant access to diagnostic modes, stream logs, agent status monitors, and configuration options without drowning the shell in clutter.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsevern-trace-preview.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fsevern-trace-preview.png" alt="severn-trace neon UI mockup" width="800" height="400"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Zero-Dependency Footprint
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To keep execution instant on low-overhead terminals like Termux, &lt;code&gt;severn-trace&lt;/code&gt; uses pure Python standard libraries for core routines. No heavy runtime overhead, no native C compilation dependencies—just clone, install, and run.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Cross-Platform Parity
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Whether you run &lt;code&gt;severn&lt;/code&gt; on Ubuntu under WSL or in a phone shell via Termux, you get the exact same interface, diagnostic engine, and output formatting.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🛠 Project Architecture (&lt;code&gt;pyproject.toml&lt;/code&gt;)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Under the hood, &lt;code&gt;severn-trace&lt;/code&gt; leverages modern Python packaging standards with &lt;code&gt;setuptools&lt;/code&gt; as the build backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the exact configuration structure used in &lt;code&gt;pyproject.toml&lt;/code&gt; for &lt;code&gt;0.1.2&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;
toml
[build-system]
requires = ["setuptools&amp;gt;=61.0"]
build-backend = "setuptools.build_meta"

[project]
name = "severn-trace"
version = "0.1.2"
description = "Zero-dependency diagnostic tool for AI agents"
readme = "README.md"
requires-python = "&amp;gt;=3.8"
dependencies = []

[project.scripts]
severn = "severn.cli:main"
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week3-2026-10-19"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 3: Slow and Steady&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;🦥 &lt;strong&gt;SlothSort — A Sloth-Themed File Organizer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SlothSort is a web application designed to make file organization simpler and less tedious. The goal is to help users spend less time manually organizing files and explore how AI could make the process easier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Try the live application:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://sloth-sort-flow.base44.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://sloth-sort-flow.base44.app&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explore the source code on GitHub:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/namangupta-prog/SlothSort" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/namangupta-prog/SlothSort&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built SlothSort using Base44 and a React-based frontend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also explored Ollama integration to investigate using open-weight AI models for file-organization suggestions. However, I haven't connected and tested a real model yet, so local AI inference remains unfinished.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open innovation gives developers the freedom to explore models and tools beyond closed, hosted AI APIs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For SlothSort, a properly configured local model could eventually provide more control over how file information is processed and allow experimentation with different models. This project is an early step toward exploring those possibilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


My Agent Session
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I haven't published an agent session for this project yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Lazy Payoff
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SlothSort aims to reduce the repetitive effort involved in organizing files. I haven't measured time savings with real users yet, so testing the workflow and measuring its benefits are future goals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Prize Categories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 3: Slow and Steady.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'll add any applicable partner prize categories after verifying their requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


hf26challenge #hacktoberfest #opensource #ai
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


Clone agentest: run agentic AI checklist tests offline in CI
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shipping agents that &lt;strong&gt;plan → call tools → hand off → mutate state&lt;/strong&gt;? Chat review and LLM-as-judge miss a lot of failures: false completion, wrong tool order, privacy leaks, biased labels, flaky plans.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/srpadmanabhuni/agentest" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;agentest&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; is an Apache-2.0 open-core runner for the &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/srinivaspadmanabhuni/agentic-ai-test-checklist-110" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Agentic AI Test Checklist 110&lt;/a&gt;. Clone it and run — &lt;strong&gt;no Hugging Face account&lt;/strong&gt; required at runtime.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Quick start
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;git clone https://github.com/srpadmanabhuni/agentest.git
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd &lt;/span&gt;agentest
python &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-m&lt;/span&gt; pip &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-e&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;".[dev]"&lt;/span&gt;
agentest samples
agentest run &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--suite&lt;/span&gt; functional &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--agent&lt;/span&gt; golden
agentest run &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--suite&lt;/span&gt; orchestration &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--agent&lt;/span&gt; golden
agentest matrix
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


What you get
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Suites: functional, privacy, explainability, bias_fairness, orchestration&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;15 sample agents (10 green ladder + 5 expected-fail teaching scenarios)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Deterministic oracles against a mock tool gateway&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;ISO 42001 / assurance-taxonomy aligned checklist design (not certification)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Context
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pulse: &lt;a href="https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/testing-agentic-ai-dr-srinivas-padmanabhuni-5stoc" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Testing Agentic AI&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Zenodo taxonomy: &lt;a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23587407" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23587407&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Developer guide: &lt;a href="https://github.com/srpadmanabhuni/agentest/blob/main/docs/DEVELOPER-GUIDE.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/srpadmanabhuni/agentest/blob/main/docs/DEVELOPER-GUIDE.md&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Author: Dr Srinivas Padmanabhuni · srinivas AT testAIng.com&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


FortiOS CVE-2025-61624 is scored 6.0. UNC5820 used it to root firewalls anyway
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a gap between a CVSS score and a threat actor's willingness to use a bug, and CVE-2025-61624 is a clean example. NVD records the vulnerability at 6.0 under CVSS v3.1, medium severity. CISA added it to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue on 16 October 2026 with a remediation deadline of 19 October. Palo Alto Networks attributes its use to a cluster tracked as UNC5820 and CL-STA-0954, which deployed ARM64 malware named PeerStitch against telecommunications targets.&lt;br&gt;
The score does not make the exploitation wrong. It makes the scoring model incomplete for this case, and that distinction matters for anyone whose vulnerability management workflow filters on severity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What the vulnerability is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CVE-2025-61624 is a path traversal, CWE-22, in FortiOS and in related products that share the code: FortiPAM, FortiProxy and FortiSwitchManager. The affected FortiOS ranges are 7.6.0 through 7.6.4, 7.4.0 through 7.4.9, and all versions of 7.2, 7.0 and 6.4.&lt;br&gt;
The path that matters runs through the command-line &lt;code&gt;system.auto-script&lt;/code&gt; feature. A FortiOS administrator with restricted privileges can write commands that are executed outside the intended directory. The traversal takes the write to the underlying operating system, and the result is a privilege elevation from the restricted FortiOS administrator level to root on the host.&lt;br&gt;
That framing explains the low score. The CVSS vector for the NVD entry reflects a requirement for authenticated access at some privilege level. What the score does not capture is what that access is worth on an edge firewall, and how common it is for an organisation to have accounts with that level of access that are reachable from somewhere unexpected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why a firewall is a worse target than the score suggests
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An edge firewall terminates or forwards the organisation's traffic. Root on that device means the ability to read traffic that was supposed to be inspected, alter policy, and modify the logs that would otherwise record the change. PeerStitch, as described in reporting on the campaign, stays resident in FortiOS memory and bridges communications in a way designed to evade the device's own inspection.&lt;br&gt;
That combination is what makes a medium-severity traversal worth a KEV entry. The attacker's position after exploitation is not proportional to the score of the entry point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The versions worth checking first
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Any FortiOS 6.4 or 7.0 deployment is affected without a version qualifier, which usually means an appliance that has been running the same firmware since installation. FortiProxy 7.2 and 7.0 are similarly affected across all versions, as are FortiPAM 1.0 through 1.6 except 1.7.0.&lt;br&gt;
FortiSwitchManager appears in the affected product list as well. That product is often managed by a different team than the firewalls, and it is a reasonable guess that its patch status has not been compared against the firewall's.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Investigation, given the position
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The device itself is not a trustworthy source of evidence after a root compromise. Logs on the appliance may have been altered, and an attacker with root can remove what they added.&lt;br&gt;
Two approaches remain useful.&lt;br&gt;
The first is out-of-band evidence. Network flow records, syslog forwarded to a collector the firewall does not control, and authentication logs from the directory used to administer the device all sit outside the compromised boundary and deserve review for authentication events around the dates in the campaign reporting.&lt;br&gt;
The second is configuration diffing against a known-good baseline. Auto-script entries, administrative accounts, trusted hosts and local users that do not match the last approved configuration are worth explaining one by one. The traversal writes outside the intended directory, so unexpected files on the underlying system are also relevant, though distinguishing attacker content from vendor content on a Fortinet appliance requires vendor guidance rather than guesswork.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Patch order and the reason for it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Devices reachable for management from the internet come first. Appliances that terminate remote-access VPNs come next, because their position makes them the entry point for everything behind them. Internal segmentation firewalls last, which is the order most organisations already use and the order that most often inverts itself when the patch team is different from the network team.&lt;br&gt;
After patching, restrict administrative access to management networks. The vulnerability requires an administrative session, so eliminating paths by which a restricted administrator account can be reached from an unexpected network removes the precondition rather than just the payload.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why the severity score missed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A CVSS base score describes the properties of a vulnerability in the abstract. It does not know that the affected system sits at a trust boundary, holds the organisation's traffic, and is administered by accounts whose credentials are shared across a team. Severity scores are a triage input. The KEV catalogue is a different input: evidence that someone is actually using it.&lt;br&gt;
When the two disagree, as they do here by a wide margin, the KEV entry is the one that should move the ticket.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;NVD entry for CVE-2025-61624, CWE-22, CVSS v3.1 6.0&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Fortinet advisory FG-IR-26-122 and PSIRT product coverage&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue entry, 16 October 2026&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Palo Alto Networks Unit 42 reporting on UNC5820, CL-STA-0954 and PeerStitch&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;iThome coverage of the CISA addition, 19 October 2026&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


How to Check Mac Software Compatibility Before Upgrading macOS
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A macOS upgrade can bring better security, new features, and performance improvements, but it can also break an important application, plug-in, driver, or workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before upgrading, it is worth checking whether the software you depend on still supports your Mac, your macOS version, and your hardware architecture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is especially important for users working with creative applications, audio production tools, video software, development utilities, and older professional apps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1. Check Whether the App Supports Apple Silicon or Intel
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the first things to verify is processor compatibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Modern Macs use Apple Silicon, while older Macs use Intel processors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Applications generally fall into a few categories:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Apple Silicon native&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Universal&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Intel-only&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Intel applications that can run through Rosetta&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Applications that no longer support older Intel Macs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A newer version of an application may work perfectly on Apple Silicon but drop support for older Intel hardware.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The opposite problem can also happen with older software that was never updated for modern Mac hardware.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before upgrading, confirm which architecture your current version supports.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


2. Check the Minimum macOS Requirement
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Application developers regularly change their minimum operating-system requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a new version of an app may require a newer macOS release than the version you are currently using.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You should check:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Minimum supported macOS version&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Recommended macOS version&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whether older macOS releases are still supported&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whether your current Mac can install the required macOS version&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whether a newer app release removes support for older hardware&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This matters because upgrading one application can sometimes force you to upgrade macOS as well.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


3. Check Plug-ins, Extensions, and Drivers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The main application may be compatible while one of its dependencies is not.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is common with:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Audio plug-ins&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Video plug-ins&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Browser extensions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;System extensions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Printer drivers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Storage utilities&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;VPN software&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Security tools&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hardware control software&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If your workflow depends on third-party components, check them separately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A macOS upgrade should not be treated as a single compatibility check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It is really a compatibility check for your entire workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4. Check Rosetta Requirements
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some Intel applications can continue running on Apple Silicon through Rosetta.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This can be useful for older software that has not yet been fully updated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;However, you should still verify:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whether the application works reliably through Rosetta&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whether all plug-ins also work&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whether performance is acceptable&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Whether newer versions provide native Apple Silicon support&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a native version exists, it is usually better to move toward that version over time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


5. Check Your Most Important Applications First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You do not need to investigate every small utility before an upgrade.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with the software that would cause the biggest problem if it stopped working.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Professional work applications&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Audio or video production software&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Development tools&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Design applications&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Backup software&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;VPN and security tools&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hardware-related utilities&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Critical browser extensions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This gives you a practical upgrade checklist instead of checking random applications one by one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


6. Do Not Assume the Latest Version Is Automatically Compatible
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common mistake is assuming that the newest version of an application will support every Mac and every macOS release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers often:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drop support for older macOS versions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Drop support for Intel Macs&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Change hardware requirements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Remove older plug-in systems&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Introduce new system requirements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Always check the requirements for the exact version you plan to install.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


7. Keep a Compatibility List
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you maintain several important Mac applications, keeping a simple compatibility list can save a lot of time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful table can include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Application&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Version&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Apple Silicon&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Intel&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Minimum macOS&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Notes&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;App A&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Current&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Check vendor&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Native&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;App B&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Older&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Rosetta&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Check vendor&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Upgrade recommended&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;App C&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Current&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Check vendor&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Intel support removed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can update this list before major macOS upgrades.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a broader maintained reference covering Mac software, Apple Silicon support, Intel compatibility, system requirements, and application-specific notes, see the &lt;a href="https://mactorrentpro.com/mac-software-compatibility/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Mac Software Compatibility Guide&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


8. Make a Backup Before Upgrading
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even after checking compatibility, create a proper backup before installing a major macOS update.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Software documentation can be incomplete, and real-world compatibility problems can still appear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A backup gives you a recovery option if an important application or workflow stops working.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Quick Pre-Upgrade Checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before upgrading macOS:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check your Mac model and processor&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verify your critical applications&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check minimum macOS requirements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Confirm Apple Silicon or Intel support&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check Rosetta requirements&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verify plug-ins and extensions&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check hardware drivers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Confirm backup software works&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Create a current backup&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Upgrade only after your important workflow is covered&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For more Mac software compatibility information, system requirements, version details, and maintained application references, visit &lt;a href="https://mactorrentpro.com/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MacTorrentPro&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Short answer:&lt;/strong&gt; make the CRM action contract provider-neutral before choosing a summarization API. Store the transcript reference, prompt version, model decision, and structured result as separate fields. Then a Node.js service can move routine calls between OpenAI, Claude, Gemini, an open-model endpoint, or a REST gateway without rewriting the CRM integration.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a media sales call, the valuable output is rarely a paragraph. It is a small set of actions: who owns the follow-up, what an advertiser agreed to, which date was mentioned, and what remains uncertain. That changes the engineering decision. Provider portability is useful only if those fields survive a model change and a retry. My rule is to version that contract before tuning a prompt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should cross the provider boundary?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the boundary narrow. A job record can name a transcript without exposing the transcript to every downstream component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"call_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"media-call-0187"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"transcript_uri"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"s3://private-calls/0187.txt"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"summary_schema_version"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"model_policy"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"routine"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"status"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"queued"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The URI is a private reference, not a public download link. The worker fetches the permitted input, applies the approved redaction policy, and sends only what the selected provider needs. The CRM sees a stable result contract, while the provider adapter owns message shape, model names, and response parsing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would make the result explicit rather than storing prose as the source of truth:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"call_id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"media-call-0187"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"actions"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"owner"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"account executive"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"action"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Send revised campaign brief"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"due_date"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2026-11-06"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"confidence"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"review"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"uncertainties"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"The renewal date was stated conditionally"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"provider"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"selected-at-runtime"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The schema version belongs beside the result. A prompt change can alter an action even when the transcript is identical, so the job key should include &lt;code&gt;call_id&lt;/code&gt;, prompt version, and operation version. That gives a retry a stable identity. The 24-hour default deduplication window is useful, but it does not replace a durable application key. Standard queues are at-least-once systems; the CRM write must therefore be idempotent too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should a Node.js team compare model costs for text summarization APIs?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It fails at the edges, not in the HTTP client. A team can swap a base URL in an afternoon and still be locked in through assumptions about tool calls, refusal text, token accounting, context limits, or what “confidence” means. A portable adapter has to translate those differences into the result contract and reject incomplete output before a CRM action is created.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The evaluation fixture should include short calls, overlapping speakers, no-action calls, a date such as “next Friday,” and a salesperson explicitly declining to commit. Use the same redaction rules, schema, and acceptance checks for every candidate. A fluent paragraph is not evidence that the owner and due date are correct.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is also where delivery concerns enter the design. A rate limit is expected control flow, not an exceptional story to hide in logs. A duplicate response, a delayed worker, or a model returning valid JSON with a missing owner can all create operational damage. Store the raw provider response separately from the approved CRM mutation, and require human review for the ambiguous class.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the cost-and-selection call a Python worker can use behind a Node.js application boundary. The REST surface needs no provider SDK in the service that owns the workflow. Set &lt;code&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL&lt;/code&gt; to the deployment's versioned API base before running it. The request id is stable for a cost-estimation operation; a CRM mutation should derive its idempotency key from the call and operation instead of generating a fresh value per retry.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hashlib&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="n"&gt;BASE_URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;environ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rstrip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;API_KEY&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;os&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;environ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;post_json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;operation_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;request_key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;hashlib&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sha256&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;operation_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;encode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;utf-8&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;hexdigest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Idempotency-Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request_key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;requests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;BASE_URL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;timeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status_code&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;retry_after&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;Retry-After&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;float&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;retry_after&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retry_after&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sleep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RuntimeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sa"&gt;f&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;status_code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;raise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RuntimeError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;rate limit retry budget exhausted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;


&lt;span class="n"&gt;comparison&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;post_json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/v1/ai/cost/compare&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;capability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;chat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;media-call-0187:cost-compare:1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;estimate&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;post_json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;/v1/ai/cost/estimate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;deepseek-v4.1-flash&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;input_tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;12000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;output_tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;900&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;media-call-0187:cost-estimate:1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;comparison&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;comparison&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;estimate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;estimate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The comparison answers which models are available at the time of the request. The estimate answers what a planned batch may consume. Keep those decisions in the job metadata, because a model catalog is not a permanent contract; the live snapshot was dated 2026-10-23. For ordinary extraction, a lightweight model such as &lt;code&gt;deepseek-v4.1-flash&lt;/code&gt; or a similar open model is a sensible starting policy. Reserve a stronger model for conditional commitments, multi-party ambiguity, or analysis that will trigger a consequential workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Which real options fit this contract?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The candidates have different operational boundaries, so a fair comparison needs more than a price column.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where it fits&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Boundary to test&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;OpenAI API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Familiar chat-completion tooling for a Node.js team and a large ecosystem&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Model-specific output behavior and prompt assumptions still need contract tests&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Anthropic Claude&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A candidate for long, nuanced sales conversations and analytical summaries&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Message formats, model identifiers, and client conventions differ from OpenAI's&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Google Gemini API&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A practical route for teams already using Google Cloud identity and controls&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Project configuration and provider-specific controls become adapter responsibilities&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Open models through hosted or self-managed inference&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More control over deployment and data location for routine extraction&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Capacity, evaluation, upgrades, and operations move closer to the application team&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A REST routing layer&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One HTTP contract for model selection and cost inspection across a broader backend&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The routing layer still needs provider-level quality tests and capability checks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai fits the last row when a backend values a plain REST API. A Node.js service can make an HTTP request without installing or versioning a dedicated client library, and the cost comparison and estimate routes are available in the same interface. Its public discovery surface is self-describing, with schemas and runnable examples, which is useful when an adapter has to inspect capability details rather than hard-code a growing list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a second advantage beyond HTTP: one key and one bill. Infrai provides 295 routes across 20 modules under one key, so a media backend that also needs other backend capabilities can keep credential and billing reconciliation in one place instead of collecting separate keys and invoices. The self-describing discovery surface adds a practical development benefit: an adapter can inspect schemas and runnable examples without waiting for another SDK release. That reduces administrative joins and setup friction around the workflow; it does not prove that one model is best for every transcript.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limitation is concrete. Infrai is not a fit as the transcription provider for an audio-first pipeline because the current deployment does not serve ASR. Voice sessions are pending and limited to the western region, and there is no dedicated moderation endpoint; text or image moderation requires a chat model with a &lt;code&gt;json_schema&lt;/code&gt; fallback. For this article's text summarization stage, those constraints are manageable because transcription can remain outside the adapter and the CRM contract starts after text exists. A team that needs served ASR first should choose a provider that offers it and keep this text adapter optional. The trade-off is explicit: shared backend administration is worth more than a unified route for a team that needs one vendor's specialized audio stack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How should the rollout protect CRM actions?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Begin in shadow mode. Generate a proposed summary and action set, but require a person to approve the CRM write. Keep the source reference, redaction result, prompt version, selected model, estimate, response metadata, and review decision together for the organization-approved retention period. Do not put a complete sales transcript in ordinary application logs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run the same consented fixture set after each model or prompt change. Check field-level correctness: action owner, advertiser commitment, due date, uncertainty, and empty action lists. Keep a small rejection queue for outputs that are valid JSON but semantically incomplete.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The migration then becomes a policy change instead of a CRM rewrite. Add one adapter, replay the fixtures, compare the structured results, and move one queue at a time. Keep the prior provider available until duplicate-delivery tests and human review show that the new path preserves the action contract.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Choose the lightweight path for routine summaries, estimate batches before they run, and make provider replacement a property of the job record. The provider is replaceable only after the CRM action is stable.&lt;/p&gt;

